: minimizing imprisonment years. The key challenge lies in understanding how two independent instances of the same model, Sasha and Rowan, will interact in this scenario, especially with the same level of reasoning effort. I'm exploring the implications of their shared architecture and information.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Framework**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the specifics of A-FDT within the Twin Prisoner's Dilemma. I've re-examined the key table in Section 5 and am working on understanding the implications of different observations ($x$) in the A-FDT equation (4). Currently, I'm trying to figure out how $\\underline{x}$ is defined in the equation, given the prisoners' differing private information. The essay's explanation of updatelessness is now my focus.\n\n\n**Investigating the Symmetric Structure**\n\nI'm now focusing on how A-FDT handles the Twin Prisoner's Dilemma when the prisoners have different private information, but the structure is symmetric. The core question is whether the decision algorithm depends on this symmetric structure. I'm exploring whether Rowan's action is subjunctively dependent on the mathematical function being run. If the algorithm always maps this game structure to \"cooperate\", then Rowan will also cooperate. I need to verify this assumption.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10922,"completion_tokens":707,"total_tokens":11629,"cost":0.01084275,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.01084275,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0081915,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00265125},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":706,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}